# Story layout for Kanakadhāra Stotram Verses 4, 5 and 6.
# Verse 4 has no author file (built from the verified KVERSES data); Verses 5 and 6 follow Amit's own commentary files.

from story_common import DISC, A

PART = {
 # DRAFT: no author file for this verse
 4: dict(
  intro=["In the first three verses, Śaṅkarācārya followed the glance of Mahalakshmi: its sidelong approach, its repeated going and returning, and its great power.",
         "In Verse 4, he comes close and looks at the eye itself: the lowered lids, the lashes, the pupil, and the love behind them. After the power of the glance, we see its tenderness. Only at the very end does the wish arrive."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“May the eye of the consort of him who rests on the serpent become a source of prosperity for me: the eye that, having come upon Mukunda with joy, the root of bliss, gazes at him unblinking and ruled by love, half-closed, with its pupil and lashes held in a narrowed, sidelong look.”"),
   ("sec","In simple words",["“O Mother, I see how you look at Vishnu: with a joy so deep that your eyes are half-closed and your gaze does not move away from him. May that same loving look turn towards me and help my life flourish.”","The first three lines paint a picture of love. The last line is the quiet request."]),
   ("sec","What the verse actually says",["The subject of the verse is her eye. Everything before the last line describes it: the half-closed lids, the steady look, the pupil and lashes in their narrowed pose.","Only in the last line does the wish arrive: <em>bhūtyai bhavet mama</em>, “may it be for my prosperity”. The glance of her eye is what Śaṅkarācārya hopes will come to him."]),
   ("rows","The four images in the verse",[
    ("eye","First – Āmīlita-akṣam: the half-closed eyes",["<em>Mīlita</em> is closed; the prefix <em>ā</em> softens it to “a little, partly”, so <em>āmīlita</em> is half-shut. <em>Akṣa</em> is the eye. The lids are lowered but not closed: the look of someone taking in something dear.","The long compound <em>ākekara-sthita-kanīnika-pakṣma-netram</em> completes the picture: the eye in which the pupil (<em>kanīnikā</em>) and the lashes (<em>pakṣma</em>) stay held in a soft, narrowed, sidelong pose."],"What small gesture of mine shows what I feel?"),
    ("bud","Second – Ānanda-kandam: the root of bliss",["<em>Ānanda</em> is bliss; <em>kanda</em> is a bulb or tuber, the underground root from which a plant springs. So Mukunda is not simply happy: he is the source from which bliss grows.","<em>Mukunda</em> is a name of Vishnu, which tradition explains as “giver of <em>mukti</em>” (liberation). She comes upon him <em>mudā</em>, with joy."],"Do I wait for a future event to be happy, or look for the source of joy now?"),
    ("flame","Third – Animeṣam, Anaṅga-tantram: the unblinking gaze, ruled by Love",["<em>Nimeṣa</em> is a blink; <em>a-</em> negates it. Strictly, the word sits in the same case as <em>Mukundam</em>, but translators commonly carry it over to her gaze, which does not turn away. Both readings give the same scene: one steady, absorbed look.","<em>Anaṅga</em> means “without a body”, the name of Kāma, the god of love. <em>Tantra</em> here is rule or sway, so the compound reads “governed by Love”. Commentators handle this compound in slightly different ways; the plain sense is the look of someone held by love."],"What would I notice if I gave one thing my full attention today?"),
    ("sprout","Fourth – Bhūtyai bhavet mama: the prayer for flourishing",["<em>Bhūti</em> comes from <em>bhū</em>, to be or to become: growth, well-being, prosperity. <em>Bhavet</em> is the wishing form of <em>bhū</em>, “may it be, may it become”. Together: “may it become, for my prosperity.”","The one whose eye this is, is named last: <em>bhujaṅga-śayāṅganā</em>, the consort of him who rests on the serpent, that is, of Vishnu on Śeṣa."],"What does a flourishing life look like for me?"),
   ]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in the verse",["Look at what Śaṅkarācārya chooses to show. Not Vishnu’s crown, not the splendour of his heaven, not the ornaments she wears. He shows the smallest movement of her eye: the lowered lids, the lashes, the pupil held to one side.","Deep affection is often shown this way, not in words but in how we look at someone. <em>Animeṣam</em>, “unblinking”, makes the look feel continuous; <em>mudā</em>, “with joy”, gives it warmth. A tiny physical detail carries a whole world of feeling."]),
   ("sec","Reading the compounds",["The first two lines are a chain of describing words in one case: <em>āmīlitākṣam</em>, <em>ānanda-kandam</em>, <em>animeṣam</em>, <em>anaṅga-tantram</em>. Sanskrit can pile up such words so that they flavour one another without a single verb between them.","Translators differ on which word describes her and which describes him, and the difference is noted in the word list. The picture is the same either way: a gaze that rests, steady and glad, on the source of joy."]),
   ("sec","The beauty of attention",["The verse says she looks at Mukunda with joy and without turning away. What we may reflect on, beyond the verse, is how rarely we give anything that kind of attention. We divide ourselves between work, worry and the next task.","This is a reflection inspired by the verse, not a line in it: could our work, or a conversation with someone we love, be better for even five minutes of whole attention?"]),
   ("sec","Bliss as a source, not a prize",["Mukunda is <em>ānanda-kanda</em>, the root of bliss. Śaṅkarācārya turns our eyes towards the source of joy, not towards the things we hope will make us happy.","We often think, “When I earn more or solve this problem, then I will be happy.” The verse suggests a different question: while I work for what I need, can I also notice the sources of peace already in my life? This does not ask us to give up ambition. It asks us not to hang all our happiness on the next achievement."]),
   ("sec","What <em>bhūti</em> can mean",["The verse asks for <em>bhūti</em>: prosperity, flourishing, well-being. For a householder this certainly includes a steady income, the means to support a family and freedom from want.","As a wider reflection it may also include sound judgement, healthy relationships, peace of mind and the wisdom to use what we have. These wider meanings are reflections on the word; the Sanskrit itself says only prosperity and flourishing."]),
   ("sec","Why ask after describing love?",["Śaṅkarācārya does not first call her a giver of wealth. He first shows her in a moment of loving absorption, and only then asks.","One way to read the order is that prosperity is sought in the presence of grace and joy, not as a possession cut off from them. The verse does not promise that reciting it brings wealth. It offers a devotional ideal: ask for well-being while keeping the heart turned towards something deeper than possessions."]),
   ("table","How this verse follows Verses 1 to 3",["Verse","What we see of the glance"],[
    ["1","Its sidelong approach"],
    ["2","Its repeated going and returning"],
    ["3","Its great power"],
    ["4","Its tenderness: the eye itself, and the love behind it"],
   ],["After the power of the glance, we see its tenderness. This is a reading of the progression, not something the verse states."]),
   ("chips","Three Sanskrit words to keep",[("Mudā","joy; doing a thing with a glad heart"),("Ānanda-kanda","the root of bliss, the source from which joy springs"),("Bhūti","flourishing: life that grows and prospers")]),
   ("note","How to pronounce the key words",["Āmīlitākṣam: aa-mee-li-taak-sham. Adhigamya: a-dhi-gam-ya. Mudā: mu-daa. Mukundam: mu-kun-dam. Ānanda-kandam: aa-nan-da-kan-dam. Animeṣam: a-ni-me-sham. Anaṅga-tantram: a-nang-ga-tan-tram. Ākekara: aa-ke-ka-ra. Kanīnika: ka-nee-ni-ka. Pakṣma: paksh-ma. Bhūtyai: bhoot-yai. Bhujaṅga: bhu-jan-ga. Read slowly and let each line settle."]),
   ("next","Verse 5",["From the eye itself, "+A(5)+" moves to what the glances become. Śaṅkarācārya imagines Lakshmi’s repeated loving glances as a necklace resting on Vishnu’s chest, beside the Kaustubha jewel, and then asks that this garland of grace bring auspiciousness into his own life."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Minute 1, recite: say the verse gently and without hurry, giving each line its own breath.",
              "Minute 2, picture the scene: imagine Vishnu resting on the great serpent and Lakshmi beside him, looking at him with quiet delight. You need not force a vision; let it come as it will.",
              "Minute 3, look at the eyes: rest your attention on her lowered lids and the stillness of her look. Notice how still a loving attention can be.",
              "Minute 4, reflect: ask yourself, “What deserves my whole attention today? What am I pursuing, and why?”",
              "Minute 5, offer a prayer. Say silently: “O Mother Lakshmi, may my life flourish. Give me the wisdom to recognise true happiness, the discernment to use prosperity well and the steadiness to stay grateful as I work for my goals.”"],
  closing="The verse opens with Lakshmi’s eye resting lovingly on Mukunda, the root of bliss, and ends with a humble prayer that her grace may bring flourishing to the devotee; perhaps the lesson is to seek not prosperity alone but a life in which prosperity, joy, attention and gratitude can live together." + DISC,
 ),

 5: dict(
  intro=["In the earlier verses, we explored Lakshmi’s loving glances toward Lord Vishnu. In this verse, Śaṅkarācārya paints another beautiful image: Lakshmi’s eyes, her graceful sidelong glances, and the way divine beauty seems to adorn Vishnu himself."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“May the garland of sidelong glances of Lakshmi, the Goddess who dwells in the lotus, shine like a necklace of deep-blue sapphires upon the chest of Madhujit, Lord Vishnu, where the Kaustubha jewel rests. May that garland of glances, which fulfils even the desires of Bhagavān himself, bring auspiciousness and well-being to me.”"),
   ("sec","A more poetic rendering",["“O Mother Lakshmi, Your beautiful glances form a radiant necklace around the chest of Lord Vishnu. They shine like a string of precious blue sapphires beside his divine Kaustubha jewel.","Your loving gaze is so powerful that it fulfils even the wishes of the Supreme Lord himself.","May that same beautiful garland of Your glances bring auspiciousness into my life.”"]),
   ("sec","In simple English",["Śaṅkarācārya imagines Lakshmi’s repeated loving glances as a beautiful necklace adorning Vishnu’s chest.","The necklace is not made of gold or diamonds. It is made of her loving looks.","And he prays that this divine grace may bring goodness into his own life."]),
   ("sec","A few important expressions deserve special attention",["<em>Madhujit</em>: Vishnu is called the conqueror of the demon Madhu.","<em>Kaustubha</em>: the celebrated divine jewel associated with Vishnu’s chest.","<em>Kaṭākṣa-mālā</em>: not a physical necklace, but a garland made of glances. This is the heart of the verse."]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in the verse",["This is one of my favourite images in the hymn.","Imagine Lord Vishnu standing in divine splendour. His complexion is deep and beautiful, like a dark monsoon sky. Upon his chest rests the magnificent Kaustubha jewel, radiant with its own celestial brilliance.","And beside him is Lakshmi. She looks at him. Her eyes move gently towards him, then away, and then return.","Remember the second verse? There, Śaṅkarācārya compared her repeated glances to a female bee moving around a great lotus ("+A(2)+"). Now he takes that imagery one step further. He says that her glances have become a necklace!","Imagine this: every loving glance from Lakshmi is like one precious blue sapphire. Her repeated glances form a beautiful garland, resting across Vishnu’s chest, near the Kaustubha jewel.","What an extraordinary poetic imagination! An ordinary poet might describe Lakshmi wearing a necklace of precious stones. Śaṅkarācārya turns the idea around. Lakshmi’s necklace is made of her own loving glances. The beauty is not in the ornament. The beauty is in the affection that creates it.","And then comes an astonishing expression: <em>kāmapradā bhagavato ’pi</em>. She fulfils the desires even of Bhagavān himself. Śaṅkarācārya is suggesting that her grace is not insignificant even in relation to the Supreme Lord.","The scene is intimate, yet the language is cosmic. That is the magic of this verse."]),
   ("rows","Four deeper insights",[
    ("gem","A. Grace is itself an ornament",["The verse describes Lakshmi’s glances as a necklace. A necklace is traditionally worn to enhance beauty. But here, her affection itself becomes the ornament."],"Our achievements, wealth, titles and possessions may decorate our lives. But kindness, affection, generosity and integrity are the ornaments that give life its real beauty."),
    ("flame","B. The meaning of <em>Kāmapradā</em>",["The word <em>kāmapradā</em> means the bestower of desires or fulfiller of wishes. It is tempting to interpret this only as a prayer for material wealth. But consider a broader reflection: what do we truly desire?","<ul><li>Wealth, but also security.</li><li>Success, but also fulfilment.</li><li>Recognition, but also respect.</li><li>Comfort, but also peace.</li></ul>","The verse invites us to place our desires before the Goddess with humility. It does not say that every desire must be fulfilled exactly as we imagine. We can also pray for the wisdom to recognise which desires are truly worth pursuing."],""),
    ("heart","C. Why mention that she fulfils even Bhagavān’s desires?",["This is perhaps the most intriguing part of the verse. Śaṅkarācārya places Lakshmi’s power in a relationship with Vishnu himself.","One devotional interpretation is that Lakshmi represents divine grace and compassion, inseparable from the sustaining power of Vishnu. The Supreme is not portrayed as distant or devoid of relationship. The verse celebrates the loving completeness of the divine pair.","This is a theological reflection on the imagery, rather than a claim that the verse explains a formal doctrine."],""),
    ("sprout","D. Prosperity and auspiciousness",["Notice that the final prayer is not simply for money. Śaṅkarācārya asks for <em>kalyāṇam</em>.","This word carries the sense of goodness, auspiciousness, welfare and flourishing. A life can have money and still lack <em>kalyāṇam</em>. True well-being requires more than accumulation."],""),
   ]),
   ("sec","Reflection for today",["I would like you to think about one particular question: if Lakshmi’s garland is made of loving glances rather than precious stones, what are the ornaments of our own lives?","Perhaps they are:","<ul><li>The blessings of our parents.</li><li>The affection of our family.</li><li>The trust of our colleagues.</li><li>The ability to help someone in need.</li><li>The satisfaction of earning honestly.</li></ul>","We spend considerable time building wealth. That is necessary and worthwhile. But let us also build a life worthy of the wealth we accumulate."]),
   ("chips","Three Sanskrit treasures to remember",[("Kaustubha","the divine jewel, a symbol of the extraordinary splendour associated with Lord Vishnu"),("Kaṭākṣa-mālā","a garland of sidelong glances, a beautiful poetic expression that transforms loving attention into an ornament"),("Kalyāṇam","auspiciousness and well-being, more than material success: the flourishing of life")]),
   ("sec","The essence of verse five",["In this verse, Śaṅkarācārya transforms Lakshmi’s loving glances into a celestial necklace adorning Lord Vishnu. He then asks that this garland of divine grace bring <em>kalyāṇam</em> into his own life.","The central thought is beautiful: the greatest ornaments of life are not always the things we possess. Sometimes, they are the love we receive, the grace we experience and the goodness we share."]),
   ("table","One connection to remember before Verse 6",["Verse","The image of her eyes"],[
    ["2","Lakshmi’s eyes move like a bee around a lotus"],
    ["4","We see the delicate expression of her eyes"],
    ["5","Her glances become a necklace of blue sapphires"],
    ["6","Her presence upon Vishnu’s dark chest becomes something even more spectacular: a flash of lightning against a gathering monsoon cloud"],
   ],["There is a lovely continuity in the imagery, from the bee, to the eye, to the necklace, and now to the lightning."]),
   ("next","Verse 6",[A(6)+" transforms Lakshmi’s presence upon Vishnu’s dark chest into something even more spectacular: a flash of lightning against a gathering monsoon cloud."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Five-minute meditation, The Garland of Grace. Minute 1, recitation: recite the verse slowly, particularly the phrase kāmapradā bhagavato ’pi kaṭākṣa-mālā.",
              "Minute 2, visualisation: picture the Kaustubha jewel shining upon Vishnu’s chest. Imagine Lakshmi’s loving glances forming a beautiful garland around it.",
              "Minute 3, contemplation: think of the people whose affection, guidance and kindness have enriched your life.",
              "Minute 4, examine your desires: ask yourself, “Are my ambitions leading me towards a better life, or merely towards more possessions?”",
              "Minute 5, prayer: “O Mother Lakshmi, may my desires be guided by wisdom. May my prosperity bring happiness not only to me, but also to those whose lives touch mine. May my life be filled with kalyāṇam.”"],
  closing="The greatest ornaments of life are not always the things we possess; sometimes they are the love we receive, the grace we experience and the goodness we share." + DISC,
 ),

 6: dict(
  intro=["In verse five, we saw Lakshmi’s loving glances transform into a necklace of blue sapphires upon Lord Vishnu’s chest.",
         "Now, in verse six, Śaṅkarācārya gives us a magnificent visual image: Goddess Lakshmi shining like a flash of lightning against the dark body of Lord Vishnu, who resembles a rain-bearing monsoon cloud.",
         "It is a beautiful meeting of light and darkness, of nature and divinity. Let us decode it carefully."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“May the magnificent form of the daughter of Bhṛgu, the revered Mother of all the worlds, who shines like a maiden of lightning upon the beautiful, dark, rain-cloud-like chest of Kaiṭabhāri, Lord Vishnu, bestow auspicious blessings upon me.”"),
   ("sec","The literary meaning",["“O Mother Lakshmi, You shine upon the dark and beautiful chest of Lord Vishnu like a brilliant streak of lightning across a monsoon cloud.","You are the magnificent and venerable Mother of all creation, the beloved daughter of Sage Bhṛgu.","May Your divine presence bring auspiciousness, goodness and prosperity into my life.”"]),
   ("sec","In simple English",["Śaṅkarācārya compares Vishnu’s dark complexion to a beautiful rain-filled cloud. Lakshmi, shining upon him, resembles a flash of lightning against that cloud.","He then remembers her universal identity: she is not merely Vishnu’s beloved consort, but the revered Mother of all worlds.","And he prays: “O Mother of the Universe, may Your magnificent presence bring goodness and auspiciousness into my life.”"]),
   ("sec","Two expressions deserve particular attention",["<em>Kaiṭabhāreḥ</em>: Kaiṭabha was a demon associated with the traditional account of Vishnu’s cosmic victory. The name identifies Vishnu as the slayer of Kaiṭabha.","<em>Bhārgava-nandanāyāḥ</em>: Lakshmi is called the daughter of Bhṛgu. This is one of her traditional names and associations."]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in the verse",["Now imagine the scene. The sky is filled with enormous dark monsoon clouds. They are deep blue, almost black, carrying the promise of rain. There is a certain majesty in their darkness.","Śaṅkarācārya asks us to see Lord Vishnu in those clouds. He is the great sustainer, calm and majestic, with a complexion like the rain-filled sky.","And then, suddenly, there is light! A brilliant streak of lightning illuminates the darkness. That lightning is Lakshmi.","But notice something extraordinary: Śaṅkarācārya does not simply say that Lakshmi shines beside Vishnu. He compares her to a <em>taḍid-aṅganā</em>, a maiden of lightning. What a beautiful personification! Lightning becomes a graceful woman, dancing across the dark sky. And the dark sky becomes the divine body of Vishnu.","Imagine the contrast:","<ul><li>The deep, dark blue of Vishnu.</li><li>The radiant golden brilliance of Lakshmi.</li><li>The sudden illumination that makes the entire sky glow.</li></ul>","For a moment, the darkness itself becomes beautiful because the lightning reveals its splendour.","And then Śaṅkarācārya expands the scene beyond the two divine figures. He calls Lakshmi <em>mātuḥ samasta-jagatām</em>, the Mother of all the worlds. We have moved from a beautiful intimate image of two divine beings to a cosmic vision of the Mother of the entire universe. That is the extraordinary journey of this verse. A flash of lightning becomes the Mother of creation.","And the prayer is simple: may that divine radiance illuminate my life too."]),
   ("rows","Four deeper insights",[
    ("cloud","A. Light and darkness are not always enemies",["<b>What the verse says:</b> Lakshmi shines like lightning against Vishnu’s dark, rain-bearing-cloud-like form.","<b>What we may contemplate:</b> Darkness here is not portrayed as something evil. It is the majestic darkness of a cloud carrying rain. The cloud gives water; the lightning reveals its beauty.","In our lives, difficult periods can sometimes carry the possibility of renewal. We should not romanticize suffering, but neither should we assume that every dark period is without purpose. Sometimes a difficult season teaches us patience, resilience and discernment."],""),
    ("lotus","B. Lakshmi is more than material wealth",["<b>What the verse says:</b> She is the Mother of all worlds, <em>samasta-jagatām mātā</em>.","This is a much larger description than simply calling her the Goddess of wealth. As a devotional reflection, Lakshmi represents the sustaining and nourishing abundance of life. Think of the different forms of abundance:","<ul><li><em>Dhana</em>: material wealth.</li><li><em>Dhānya</em>: food and nourishment.</li><li><em>Vidyā</em>: knowledge.</li><li><em>Dhairya</em>: courage and patience.</li><li><em>Santāna</em>: family continuity.</li><li><em>Kīrti</em>: honour and good reputation.</li></ul>","These are traditional categories of prosperity often associated with Lakshmi worship. They help us appreciate why her role is understood so broadly."],""),
    ("sprout","C. The meaning of <em>Bhārgava-nandanā</em>",["Why does Śaṅkarācārya specifically call Lakshmi the daughter of Bhṛgu? The name connects the Goddess with an ancient lineage of Vedic tradition.","It also gives the prayer a personal devotional quality. Śaṅkarācārya is not invoking an abstract idea of prosperity. He is addressing a revered divine Mother through one of her traditional identities."],""),
    ("hands","D. From beauty to blessing",["The earlier verses repeatedly described Lakshmi’s eyes, glances and affection. Here, Śaṅkarācārya makes a larger prayer: <em>bhadrāṇi me diśatu</em>, may she grant me auspiciousness.","This is a meaningful change. He is no longer asking only for a moment of her glance. He asks for the goodness that her presence can bring into life.","It reminds us that the purpose of prayer is not simply to ask for things, but also to become receptive to a life of greater goodness."],""),
   ]),
   ("sec","Reflection for today",["I want to leave you with a thought arising from the image of the monsoon cloud and lightning. A cloud can be enormous, powerful and majestic. Yet a single flash of lightning can illuminate the entire sky. Similarly, sometimes a small moment of wisdom can change the way we see an enormous problem.","Consider these questions:","<ol><li>Where in my life do I need greater clarity?</li><li>Am I measuring prosperity only by financial growth?</li><li>Can I use my success to nourish and support others, just as rain nourishes the earth?</li></ol>","The third question is particularly important. Wealth becomes truly meaningful when it creates something good beyond ourselves."]),
   ("chips","Three Sanskrit treasures to remember",[("Taḍit","lightning, a flash of illumination; in this verse, the poetic image of Lakshmi’s radiant presence"),("Mūrtiḥ","form or manifestation, the visible expression of something divine or sacred"),("Bhadrāṇi","auspicious blessings and goodness; not merely good fortune, but the flourishing and well-being of life")]),
   ("sec","The essence of verse six",["In this verse, Śaṅkarācārya gives us one of the most memorable images in the hymn: Lakshmi is the lightning. Vishnu is the magnificent monsoon cloud. Together, they reveal the beauty of divine abundance.","He then addresses Lakshmi as the Mother of all worlds and asks her to grant auspiciousness.","The central thought: true prosperity is not merely the accumulation of wealth. It is the arrival of light, wisdom, nourishment and goodness in our lives."]),
   ("next","Verse 7",["A glimpse of verse seven: it takes us in a fascinating direction. Śaṅkarācārya now describes how even Manmatha (Kāma, the god of love) obtained a place in the heart of Lord Vishnu through the influence of Lakshmi’s glance. He then makes a personal prayer that her gentle, languid half-glance may fall upon him.","In "+A(7)+" we will see how the hymn moves from the cosmic image of lightning to the delicate power of a single glance."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Five-minute meditation, The Light of the Mother: a quiet contemplation after reciting verse six. Minute 1, recitation: recite the verse slowly, paying particular attention to bhadrāṇi me diśatu Bhārgava-nandanāyāḥ.",
              "Minute 2, visualisation: imagine a vast monsoon sky, dark and majestic. A beautiful flash of golden lightning illuminates it.",
              "Minute 3, contemplation: visualise Lakshmi as the radiant Mother of the universe. Let the image represent nourishment, compassion and abundance.",
              "Minute 4, personal reflection: think about one difficult situation in your life. Ask yourself what wisdom, patience or courage might help you navigate it.",
              "Minute 5, prayer: “O Mother of all worlds, may Your light guide my decisions. May there be prosperity in my home, wisdom in my work, compassion in my heart and auspiciousness in all that I undertake.”"],
  closing="True prosperity is not merely the accumulation of wealth; it is the arrival of light, wisdom, nourishment and goodness in our lives." + DISC,
 ),
}
